# TopoPilot 2

## Installation

TopoPilot is installed as a Python library using pip and can be installed in any Python environment that has access to ParaView and the Topology Toolkit. As you may know, ParaView's Python bindings cannot be installed with pip, leading to various headaches.

We provide instructions to install TopoPilot using Anaconda as well as Python virtual environments. The Anaconda version has no prerequisites (other than Anaconda) and is generally bette behaved. The Python virtual environment version requires that you already have ParaView and TTK installed on your machine, while the Anaconda version does not. It also will be more annoying to work with (see below)

---

### Installing outside an environment (not recommended)

#### Prerequisites:
- ParaView python bindings active in your base environment
- The Topology Toolkit installed in ParaView (does not need to be loaded by default)

#### Instructions
```pip3 install -e TopoPilot2```

---

### Installing with Anaconda

#### Prerequisites:
- Anaconda

#### Instructions

First, create a conda environment that contains TTK using:

```conda create -n <env_name> -c conda-forge topologytoolkit```

Where ```<env_name>``` should be replaced by the name of the environment that you are creating. Then, activate the environment using:

```conda activate <env_name>```

Finally, install TopoPilot by running

```pip3 install -e path/to/TopoPilot2```

where the path should point to the same folder that contains this README.md.

---

### Installing with Python virtual environments

#### Prerequisites:
- ParaView python bindings active in your base environment
- The Topology Toolkit installed in ParaView (does not need to be loaded by default)
- Knowledge of how Python virtual environments work

#### python3 vs pvbatch tradeoffs

When running ParaView python scripts, a black window the size of your entire screen will appear when you run the script using ```python3```. To make this disappear, you can use ```pvbatch``` instead. However, ```pvbatch``` cannot be used to open a REPL.

Unfortunately, virtual environments can only support the use of one of ```python3``` or ```pvbatch``` and not both. Thus, you will need to select which one you would like to use. If you use ```python3``` an annoying window will open .

#### Creating a virtual environment with ParaView

If you already have an environment that you want to work with (with either ```python3``` or ```pvbatch``` then feel free to move on to the next step.)

To make the environment for ```python3``` run:

```python3 -m venv <name_of_venv> --system-site-packages```

where you can replace ```<name_of_venv>``` with the desired virtual environment name. Similarly, to make the environment for ```pvbatch```, run:

```pvbatch -m venv <name_of_venv> --system-site-packages```.

Then, activate the environment by running ```source <name_of_venv>```

This requires you to include all of the packages in your base environment in the virtual environment. If you were not careful with managing your base environment, this can lead to compatability issues. However, to my knowledge, this is the only way to transfer ParaView's python bindings from your base environment to the new environment.

#### Installing into the virtual environment

Run the following command:

```python3 -m pip install -e path/to/TopoPilot2```

where the path should point to the same folder that contains this README.md.

**Note:** if you created your environment with ```pvbatch``` and you instead just do ```pip install``` (without the ```python3 -m```) then this will run your system's ```pip``` and attempt to install to the base environment (which defeats the purpose of creating this virtual environment).

---

## Usage

### Model provider configuration

TopoPilot supports the OpenAI and Anthropic APIs. Select an OpenAI or Claude
model with `topopilot.config.set_llm(...)`; the verifier can be selected
independently with `set_verifier_llm(...)`. For example:

```python
from topopilot.api import LLM
from topopilot.config import set_llm, set_verifier_llm

set_llm(LLM.claudeSonnet5Medium)
set_verifier_llm(LLM.gpt56TerraLowReasoning)
```

GPT-5.6 Sol, Terra, and Luna each support explicit `NoReasoning`,
`LowReasoning`, `MediumReasoning`, `HighReasoning`, `XHighReasoning`, and
`MaxReasoning` variants. For example, `LLM.gpt56TerraMediumReasoning` selects
`gpt-5.6-terra` with `medium` reasoning effort. The corresponding configuration
name is `gpt-5.6-terra-medium`; the other names follow the same pattern.

Claude Sonnet 5 can be selected with explicit `High`, `Medium`, or `Low`
effort using `LLM.claudeSonnet5High`, `LLM.claudeSonnet5Medium`, or
`LLM.claudeSonnet5Low`. `LLM.claudeSonnet5` remains available and uses the
provider default effort.

Claude Haiku 4.5 can be used with thinking explicitly disabled via
`LLM.claudeHaiku45NoThinking` (`claude-haiku-4-5-no-thinking`).
Claude Sonnet 5 has an equivalent `LLM.claudeSonnet5NoThinking` option
(`claude-sonnet-5-no-thinking`).

Set the standard provider environment variable before launching TopoPilot:

```bash
export OPENAI_API_KEY="..."
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="..."
```

Only the provider selected for the main model or verifier needs to be
configured. For local testing, a key can instead be placed by itself in
`openai_api_key.txt` or `anthropic_api_key.txt` next to `TopoPilot2Library`:

```text
TopoPilot2/
├── openai_api_key.txt
├── anthropic_api_key.txt
└── TopoPilot2Library/
```

Key files are intended only for local testing and should not be committed.
Existing session-level `keys.json` files remain supported using the keys
`"OpenAI"` and `"Anthropic"`.

### Launching

To run both the data browser and MCP server, with installation environment activated, run the following in your command line:

```TopoPilot```

To run just the viewer or just the server, you can instead run ```TopoPilot-Client``` or ```TopoPilot-Server```. If you are only using TopoPilot to perform and save computations, and do not need to visualize anything, then you only need to run the MCP server.

If you used a Python virtual environment and used ```python3``` as your base, you may encounter the following error from ```pvbatch```:

```/usr/bin/pvbatch: Error while finding module specification for 'topopilot.ui.client' (ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'topopilot')```

If that occurs, then instead of ```TopoPilot```, run ```TopoPilot-NoBatch```, and instead of ```TopoPilot-Client``` run ```TopoPilot-Client-NoBatch```.

### Connecting the MCP server

Our MCP server uses the endpoint ```localhost:8000/mcp``` and requires a host header of ```localhost:8000```.

The instructions for connecting to an MCP server are different for each LLM and are fairly involved. Please see the model specific instructions for how to connect it.
